Recent analyses of lepton flavor universality (LFU) violating $b 	o c$ transitions have led to several individual observations that could have significant implications depending on their underlying causes. In the context of the $U_1$ leptoquark model, which is capable of explaining these anomalies while preserving LFU, we investigate the impact on the $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay. Specifically, we assess how a signal in $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ would be affected by the presence of a $b 	o c$ LFU violating signal at LHCb and the Large Hadron Collider. To this end, we determine the range of relevant parameters and interacting scalar and fermion masses, and subsequently, the associated effects on decay rates and branching ratios. We observe that the $b 	o c$ signal can significantly impact the $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay at LHCb, leading to a possible alternative explanation for a potential anomaly in the $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ data collected so far, and we outline the experimental strategies required to verify the $U_1$ leptoquark model through $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay at the Large Hadron Collider and future colliders.
